data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetProperty` value argument must be a lambda expression

In EF Core 10, `SetProperty`'s second argument now **requires** `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>` — a bare `Expression.Constant(...)` no longer works.

**Fix:** Wrap the constant in a lambda:

```csharp
var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
var valueParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b2");

setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>>(
    Expression.Call(
        instance: setters.Body,
        methodName: nameof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>.SetProperty),
        typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
        arguments: new Expression[]
        {
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
            // Wrap constant in a lambda instead of bare Expression.Constant
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Constant("foo"), valueParameter)
        }),
    setters.Parameters);
```

## 2. Complex property column naming now includes the navigation prefix

EF Core 10 changed the default column naming convention: complex property columns are prefixed with the navigation name (e.g., `ShippingAddress_Street`, `BillingAddress_Street`). If your existing schema uses unprefixed names, migrations will try to rename columns.

**Fix:** Explicitly configure column names to match your existing schema:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, sa =>
    {
        sa.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street");
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ba =>
    {
        ba.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street");
    });
});

// For nested: configure each level
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb =>
    {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ba =>
        {
            ba.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

Or, if you're creating the schema fresh, just generate a new migration and let EF use the new defaults.

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` gained a `CommandSource` parameter

In EF Core 10, diagnostic methods like `CommandReaderExecuting` added a `CommandSource` parameter to their signatures.

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // <-- new required parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

---

**Summary:** Update all manual expression trees to use value lambdas, audit your complex property column mappings (or regenerate migrations), and add `CommandSource` to any `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` implementations.
